The layout of the source is kept, because it is what makes the
includes work unchanged: the lib sources reach "diff_internal.h" and
"diff_debug.h" beside themselves, and <arraylist.h> and <diff_main.h>
through an include path. The two include paths the Makefile passes
are the two FreeBSD's own lib/libdiff/Makefile passes.

Not carried: diff_output.c, diff_output_plain.c, diff_output_unidiff.c,
diff_output_edscript.c and include/diff_output.h, which print diffs --
the merge walks the chunk list itself; compat/strlcpy.c, strlcat.c,
merge.c and getprogname_linux.c, which only the output side and the
sample program need; the test suite and the makefiles.

Rules
-----

Nothing in this directory is edited locally, ever. Not a byte, not a
trailing space. Every adaptation lives in the picker's own files, so
that a refresh stays a plain copy. A carried file that will not build
under the tool's warning set gets a flag on its object's line in the
Makefile, with a comment naming the file and the diagnostic, and the
file itself is left alone.

Every carried file is ASCII, so the directory stays inside the ASCII
check that tools/gate.sh runs over the tree. It is exempt from cstyle
and from tools/xcheck-freebsd.sh: foreign code keeps its own style,
and the FreeBSD build of it is the box's to confirm.

Known defects, to report upstream
---------------------------------

Found by the code review of 2026-09-11 (sprints/sprint-6/
code-review-2026-09-11.md, findings G1 and G7) and verified against
this copy, at the line numbers this commit has. None of them is fixed
here: the rule above is the rule. They go to the Game of Trees project
and to FreeBSD, and a refresh is what brings the fixes back.

Four allocation sites in lib/diff_patience.c, all of them inside
diff_algo_patience, which our own configuration puts at the top of the
nest so that every diff enters it:

	387	calloc of one struct atom_patience per line of the
		left side, unchecked; published into left->algo_data
		at :397 and indexed by the PATIENCE macro at :56.
	389	the same for the right side, into right->algo_data
		at :398.
	504	recallocarray of the LCS array, unchecked, with
		&lcs[lcs_count - 1] formed and written through at
		:505 in the loop below it.
	432	a checked allocation whose "return ENOMEM" at :435
		goes around free_and_exit, leaking both patience
		tables and leaving root->current set.

The first three are dereferenced at once when the allocation fails.
Reproduced with an allocator that refuses above a cap: the picker dies
of SIGSEGV on a 100000-line merge where the library's own header
promises a refusal. The fix is four lines -- check the two callocs and
the lcs allocation, and turn the return at :435 into "rc = ENOMEM;
goto free_and_exit;".

Our own guard against reaching them is ZR_M3_MAXLINES in
../merge.h, a line ceiling refused in zr_m3_open before libdiff is
asked for anything. It is a guard and not a fix.

Two undefined-behaviour sites in lib/diff_main.c, inside
diff_algo_none:

	495	&r[r_idx] where the right side's atom array head is
		NULL and r_idx is 0 (the minus chunk, a whole-file
		deletion).
	504	&l[l_idx] where the left side's head is NULL and
		l_idx is 0 (the plus chunk, an empty base).

A zero offset from a null pointer is undefined in C and defined by
every compiler this is built with, so nothing misbehaves today; an
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer build of the merge reports these two and
nothing else. Worth carrying upstream with the four above.

Refresh
-------

	sh tools/refresh-libdiff.sh [FREEBSD_TREE] [COMMIT]

with FREEBSD_TREE defaulting to $FREEBSD_SRC or /usr/src and COMMIT
to the commit that tree is at. Then make check: the battery is what
says whether the merge's answers moved. One commit for the refresh.
